WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.567
-Mr. Big Shot.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.334
Came back to see us, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:05.400
-We missed you, bud.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.167
I miss seeing you
every day, man.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:10.033
-Yeah. You know, I've missed
everyone around here too, man.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.634
All the guys on the floor
and the audience.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:14.634
You know, definitely --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.434
Yeah.

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:19.801
The nurses, hair and make-up,

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:22.267
you know,
everyone behind the curtain,

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:24.501
you know,
definitely the band and stuff.

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:25.801
-Oh.

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:27.601
-Security.
All the security guys.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:31.200
-Yeah, they're pretty cool.
-Yeah, I miss all those guys.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.634
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:34.300
-Well, welcome back to the show.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.601
Congrats on "Black No More."

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:42.234
You not only wrote the songs,
but you star in it, too.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:43.334
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:44.968
-But you've been
working on this for years.

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:46.334
How long now? Six?

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:49.067
-You know, six, seven years,
I feel like, yeah.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:50.834
-I don't know if
people understand how much time

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.701
goes into making these shows.

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:55.334
It is awesome, by the way, bud.
-Thank you. Thanks, man.

00:00:55.334 --> 00:00:56.434
-How did you get
started on this?

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:58.267
Who told you about this?

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:01.534
-About seven years or so ago,
John Ridley,

00:01:01.534 --> 00:01:04.968
who is the writer of this book,

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:08.400
and Scott Elliott,
who directed the show,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.901
approached us
and, you know, said,

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:13.167
"You know, I know this is going
to sound crazy, but, you know,

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.968
we're thinking that you might
want to, you know,

00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:16.200
embark on this endeavor
with us."

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.167
-Did they think it
was going to be a musical?

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.067
-Well, originally,
I don't think Ridley thought

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.601
it was going to be a musical.

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:23.801
I think he thought
it would be anything but.

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:27.400
So he -- After he exhausted
all options,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.634
he's like,
"You know, I'll just --

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:31.167
I'll do a musical,
and why don't we get The Roots."

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:32.434
No.

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:35.534
-Honestly, it's some of the best
lyrics you'll ever hear.

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:38.367
And it's so much
and it's so you.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:41.100
-I think it's densely packed
and I think the audience,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.033
you know, is going to feel --
I think you'll experience

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:47.868
the full range of emotions,
so to speak.

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:49.300
You'll laugh. You'll cry.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.601
You'll...
[ Gasps ]

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.701
You'll be surprised.
-Oh, there's twists and turns.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:55.634
I mean, I know you've done this
now a bunch of times,

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:57.901
but to have these trained

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.334
Broadway singers
sing your stuff...

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:00.601
-Yeah.

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:02.300
-...how does that feel
to have somebody do that?

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.033
-It's surreal.
It's surreal for me, man.

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:07.868
It's -- You know,
I can't really explain it.

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:10.334
But I definitely have --
I mean, not that I didn't

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:13.567
already have, you know, just
the highest level of respect

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.767
and, you know,
appreciation for what it is

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:17.901
that people do in
the world of theater,

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:19.334
but, you know,
for musical theater

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.801
and people who are out there
every day, you know,

00:02:21.801 --> 00:02:25.000
doing eight, nine shows a week,
it's just --

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.567
you know, just kudos.

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:28.000
You know what I mean?
The utmost respect.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.434
-That's why -- But I also --

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:30.801
I love you're always
doing something different.

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:33.934
And this one is
definitely a game changer.

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.767
Do you want to tell
everyone what it's about?

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:38.868
-Yeah. I mean, you know,
it's kind of hard to nutshell,

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:41.133
but I'm going to try.

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.634
In short, the story
is about this crazy --

00:02:45.634 --> 00:02:48.934
like, a mad scientist of sorts,
played by myself,

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:53.100
who invents a machine
called the Black No More device,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.067
you know, through which
he's able to, you know, turn

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:57.701
black people white permanently.

00:02:57.701 --> 00:02:58.767
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:00.901
And, you know,
in his mind, it is --

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:03.868
you know, that's the answer
to the American race problem,

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:05.400
which, of course,
you know, it is not.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.701
-Yeah.

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:11.534
-So hilarity and tragedy
and lots of drama in between.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:13.868
-It's a genius concept.
The idea is great.

00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:16.300
"I'm going to change
black people into white people.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.734
If you want to be white,
I'm gonna to change

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:18.767
the color of your skin.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.067
Yeah, let's do it."

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:22.968
And then you see what happens
if that does happen.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.634
-Yeah.
-If that is an option.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:27.200
It's funny. It's also tragic.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.434
-Yeah.
-It's everything.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:31.133
And I want to say -- I was like,
"Hey, let me know

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:33.200
whenever you want me
to come check you out."

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.934
And so I texted you, and you go,
"Come see the show.

00:03:37.934 --> 00:03:40.734
You can come Thursday
or Sunday matinee."

00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:44.167
You go, "Those are --
My understudies are there."

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:45.400
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.434
I'm like,
"Dude, if I come see the show,

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:49.000
I want to see you in the show."

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.167
Why are you inviting me
to come see --

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:52.834
Those are the two shows
you invited me to

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:55.167
is the ones that you're not in.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:56.434
-I know, I know. Yeah.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:58.334
-"I'm coming to one of
the shows that you're in."

00:03:58.334 --> 00:03:59.968
-Yeah, you got me.

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:02.067
-Yeah. I did get you, man.

00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:04.167
And then I go,
"Yeah, let's go, man."

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:06.501
I thought it would be
a great thing.

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:08.534
I would come see you backstage,
say, "What's up, buddy?",

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:09.701
you know, "Miss you, man."

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:10.834
-Yeah, no.

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:13.100
Yeah, our paths just didn't,
you know...

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:15.868
-No, it's not what
happened at all.

00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:16.901
Our paths didn't cross.

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:18.367
I saw you,
hung out with your wife.

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:19.601
I saw your brother.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:23.567
Michelle tells me to go meet you
on 41st Street.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:25.167
-Yeah.
-I'm at the stage.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:27.300
I'm talking to everybody
and I've got the mask on.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.300
I'm doing selfies.
I'm talking about the thing.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.667
I go, "When is he
going to come out?"

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:33.100
He doesn't come out at all.
Everyone else came out.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.133
You didn't come out.
-Yeah, I exited on 42nd.

00:04:37.767 --> 00:04:41.801
[ Rimshot ]
-I stood next to a dumpster.

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.033
-Yeah, you did.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:44.400
You actually sent me,
yeah, a picture --

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.734
-A picture of me next
to a dumpster.

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:48.000
Yeah, I go,
"That's where I was."

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.667
Your wife wasn't there.
No one was there.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.300
-That's very sad.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.534
-But It's almost like
she told me where to not go

00:04:53.534 --> 00:04:55.267
so that she didn't have to
hang out with me either.

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:58.501
-She knows what's up.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.501 --> 00:04:59.734
-Dude, I was by myself.

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.434
I go, "You've got to be
kidding me, man."

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:04.234
It was --
Yeah, anyways, it was...

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:06.767
I didn't see that ending coming.

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:11.100
You know, that's another twist
and turn of my event last night.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.267
-I didn't know -- It took
a while before I realized

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.234
that you were coming backstage,
because I didn't have --

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:17.601
Unlike, you know,
when I'm working here,

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:21.801
I can't have my phone, you know,
while I'm on the job, so --

00:05:21.801 --> 00:05:23.767
-You have your phone here
when you're working here?

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:27.200
-I mean, yeah, when I'm here,
you know, I can do -- You know.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.300
I'm over there,
I'm answering e-mails.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.400
I'm online-shopping and
stuff, you know?

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.834
-Online shopping?
-Yeah.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.200
-During our show?
-Yeah, FaceTiming with my kids.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.634
We all do.

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.634
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:46.901
-You know...
-Unbelievable, man.

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:50.501
-Sorry to blow people's spot up.
-Hey!

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:51.901
-You know what, though?

00:05:51.901 --> 00:05:54.267
When you come out,
you command the stage,

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:57.100
and the audience
is just, like --

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.234
and just eating out of
the palm of your hand.

00:05:59.234 --> 00:06:00.300
It's just great.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.501
You just know how
to do it, buddy,

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:02.767
and it's impressive.

00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:04.601
And I even said this to you
in the hallway, too.

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:06.868
I said, "By the way,
the show has an intermission,

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:07.868
and everyone came back."

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:09.667
-Yeah.
-That's a good sign.

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:11.601
-That's telling, yes.

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:14.534
-I've been to plays where
you go, "I put in time.

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:15.801
I'll split."

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:18.701
-I think we've snuck out of
a couple shows together.

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:24.267
-But do you remember the first
time you performed onstage?

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:25.434
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.567
The first time I
performed onstage, you know,

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:30.234
that I can recall, I think I
was in the third grade.

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:32.367
I was in a talent show.

00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:36.167
I sang, like,
a DeBarge cover or something.

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:37.467
It wasn't just me.

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.801
It was, like, me and
another couple of my friends,

00:06:39.801 --> 00:06:40.901
and we lost.

00:06:40.901 --> 00:06:42.534
It didn't work out.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:44.701
So, I said, "You know, next
year, I'm going to come back.

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:48.601
I'm going to rap," you know, for
the fourth-grade talent show.

00:06:48.601 --> 00:06:50.434
So I came back.
I was in fourth grade.

00:06:50.434 --> 00:06:52.667
A couple of my guys were
still in third.

00:06:52.667 --> 00:06:54.901
But they -- Some of them
have gone on to greatness.

00:06:54.901 --> 00:06:58.367
You know, my original crew
for that talent show

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:00.601
was called the Crash Crew,
and the rappers were

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:04.234
myself and the emcee known
as Beanie Sigel.

00:07:04.234 --> 00:07:05.267
-No way.
-Yeah.

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:06.701
-Beanie Sigel.
Was he third grade?

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:09.334
-Yeah, he was third grade.
-You're the elder statesman.

00:07:09.334 --> 00:07:10.634
Yeah, you're like,

00:07:10.634 --> 00:07:11.667
"I don't know if I'll talk
to these third graders.

00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:12.734
I'm in fourth grade."

00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:14.634
-Yeah, I'm like, "Come on."
-Respect.

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.801
-You know he was good
if I was socializing.

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:18.734
-Yeah.
Do you remember what you rapped?

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:20.133
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:23.133
I remember I did, like, you
know, a speed-rap sort of thing.

00:07:23.133 --> 00:07:24.601
-Could you give us
a little taste?

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:25.868
You know the way
this show works.

00:07:25.868 --> 00:07:28.567
We always have a mic --
We always have a hot mic.

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:30.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:34.100
You know the way we --
Just a little taste of it.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.501
-Okay.
♪♪

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:36.667
Look...

00:07:36.667 --> 00:07:38.000
♪ Well, I'm a hip-hopper,
never-stopper ♪

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.367
♪ Rockin' all around the clock
a double "T" ♪

00:07:39.367 --> 00:07:40.534
♪ You know it's me,
I'm rockin' on the M-I-C ♪

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:42.167
♪ The money-maker,
booty-shaker ♪

00:07:42.167 --> 00:07:43.634
♪ Party rocker, non-stopper ♪
♪ Girl-taker, heartbreaker ♪

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:44.868
♪ Undercover super-lover,
debonair ♪

00:07:44.868 --> 00:07:46.067
♪ I'm everywhere, Double T,
I can't be beat ♪

00:07:46.067 --> 00:07:47.467
♪ Especially on the M-I-C ♪

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:48.701
♪ If you wanna challenge me,
well, take the mic ♪

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.000
♪ And I will beat a rocker
in a rocker ♪

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.601
♪ Make you want to
scream and shout ♪

00:07:51.601 --> 00:07:53.167
♪ This beat is oh-so-fresh ♪
♪ Double T, yeah, I'm the best ♪

00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:54.334
♪ Break-dancin',
pure romancin' ♪

00:07:54.334 --> 00:07:55.501
♪ Always fightin',
never bitin' ♪

00:07:55.501 --> 00:07:56.767
♪ Always write
and share the light ♪

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:57.901
♪ And rockin' on the mic
in the place to be ♪

00:07:57.901 --> 00:08:00.501
♪ And I go by the name of
MC Double T ♪

00:08:00.501 --> 00:08:02.501
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:08:07.167 --> 00:08:09.033
-Congrats on the show.

00:08:09.033 --> 00:08:10.167
Couldn't be happier
for you, buddy.

00:08:10.167 --> 00:08:12.534
You're brilliant.
Tariq Trotter, everybody.

00:08:12.534 --> 00:08:14.434
Go see "Black No More,"

00:08:14.434 --> 00:08:16.834
which is running now
through February 27th

00:08:16.834 --> 00:08:18.634
at the Pershing Square
Signature Center.

